###  Introduction {#sec_Introduction}

Large language models (LLMs) have demonstrated remarkable success across numerous natural language tasks, yet their ability to leverage knowledge from knowledge graphs (KGs) remains untested. We constructed a dataset from Wikidata for models to learn from, and our experiments show that T5 models trained on factual triples from this KG outperform those trained on equivalent information extracted from Wikipedia's textual corpus. This indicates that T5 models find it as easy to learn from factual triples as they do from full sentences. This method also yields state-of-the-art results on the WikiMovie questions dataset, a benchmark widely regarded as challenging for LLMs. Furthermore, our approach to injecting structured knowledge into T5 requires minimal preparation when applied to external KGs. This is particularly advantageous for industrial-scale graphs that lack accompanying text corpora. We release the WikiMovie KG T5 model and a factual-triple-based training corpus derived from the WikiMovie KG. Additionally, we provide code to
